EXT. SHELL CRATER - NIGHT

Mud. Rain. A flare drifts down over a torn field, throwing hard green light on three men in a hole.

CORPORAL AHN, 30s, crouches with a rifle across his knees. PRIVATE BOYD, 19, shivers beside him, clutching a canteen. LIEUTENANT SHARMA, 40s, lies on his back at the crater's rim with binoculars, watching.

SHARMA
Quiet.

BOYD
It's all quiet.

AHN
Boyd.

BOYD
I'm just saying.

Mortar fire thuds far off. The flare dies. Dark.

SHARMA
Two hundred meters. Wire line. A sentry.

AHN
One?

SHARMA
One.

BOYD
I'll take him.

AHN
You won't.

BOYD
I'm the best shot.

AHN
You're the loudest breather.

BOYD
That's not a reason.

SHARMA
Boyd, stay.

AHN
I'll go.

SHARMA
Wait for the next flare.

They wait. Rain taps the helmets. Boyd takes a swallow from the canteen. Ahn watches the rim, and the three of them hold their breath.

Another flare rises, hisses, bursts.

AHN
Now.

BOYD
Now!

They say it together.

AHN
Go.
^BOYD
Go.

Ahn is up and over the rim. Boyd scrambles after. Sharma slides back down into the crater, alone. He cradles the binoculars and watches them dwindle, two shapes, bent low, running toward the wire.
